Read `SKILL.md` first for authentication, payload normalization, the
save→publish/review lifecycle, and the shared safety rule. This file covers the
**专家市场**: `expert` (专家, a single agent, `plugin_type=expert`) and `squad`
(专家团, an expert team, `plugin_type=expert_team`). Both are plugins; only the
type and package shape differ.

An expert's instruction lives in the package's root `AGENTS.md`; an
expert_team is a single `AGENTS.md` describing collaboration/dispatch, and its
members and their skills/connectors are expressed as **relations** (fetch with
`--include-relations`).

## Search

```bash
octo-cli marketplace plugin-category list --scene-code default --plugin-type expert
octo-cli marketplace plugin-tag list --plugin-type expert --q "<tag>"
octo-cli marketplace plugin list --scene-code default --plugin-type expert \
  --q "<kw>" --category-id <id> --tag "<tag>" --sort updated --page 1 --page-size 20
octo-cli marketplace plugin get --plugin-id <plugin-id> --include-relations
```

Squads are identical with `--plugin-type expert_team`. `plugin list` is
page-paginated (CLI `.data` + `._pagination`); add `--mode mine` for owned
records of any visibility. Use the immutable `plugin_id`, never a name.

## Read member skills

`plugin get --include-relations` returns only the selected plugin's directly
connected live relations whose targets are visible to the caller; it is not a
transitive graph read. For an expert_team, these are its
`expert_team_expert` member relations, not each member expert's own
`expert_skill` relations. Fetch a member expert by its `plugin_id` with
`--include-relations` to inspect those skill links. Read a skill's document by
its own plugin id:

```bash
octo-cli marketplace plugin skillmd --plugin-id <skill-plugin-id>
octo-cli marketplace plugin download --plugin-id <skill-plugin-id> -o <tmp>/skill.zip
```

Relations may pin an exact `target_version_id`. Read that snapshot instead of
the live document, and inspect who still references a plugin:

```bash
octo-cli marketplace plugin version list --plugin-id <plugin-id>
octo-cli marketplace plugin version get <plugin-id> <version-id>
octo-cli marketplace plugin version download <plugin-id> <version-id> --path SKILL.md -o <tmp>/SKILL.md
octo-cli marketplace plugin dependents list <plugin-id>
```

## Create / update

Create or edit an **unpublished** draft through the unified write path. Every
save records a version snapshot but does not publish the expert or team. An
already-published Space expert/team cannot be changed with upsert; submit its
new manifest/package/relations through `plugin review-request create --data
@submission.json` so the Space policy can apply the frozen upgrade immediately
or queue it for owner/admin review. Show the target and intended change, then
continue only after explicit confirmation:

```bash
octo-cli marketplace plugin upsert --data @plugin.json
```

The `--data` body is `{"plugin":{plugin_name, plugin_type, visibility,
category_id, tags, icon, version?, manifest_json, plugin_json},
"relations":[...]}`. Both `manifest_json` and `plugin_json` are required on
every write:

- **expert**: `plugin_json` carries a root `AGENTS.md` (the instruction) and,
  when it uses connectors, a root `mcp.json` with `${VAR}` placeholders for
  consumer secrets.
- **expert_team**: `plugin_json` is a single `AGENTS.md` rendering the
  collaboration/dispatch, leader, strategies, dependencies, and permissions.
  A team's own `relations` array may contain only `expert_team_expert` entries
  (team → member expert). The leader is the member relation whose
  `data.is_leader` is true. `expert_skill` and `expert_connector` belong on each
  member expert's own upsert/review document, never directly on the team source.
  Every relation requires `target_plugin_id` and `relation_type`. Skill and member-expert relations must pin an exact released `target_version_id` when the marketplace references rollout is enabled; the server rejects a missing or unpublished pin. A connector relation must never carry a version.

Set `plugin.plugin_id` to update; omit to create. Inspect history with `plugin version list --plugin-id <id>` after a release.

> **`manifest_json` must agree with the outer `plugin` fields.** The backend
> rejects the write unless all three hold:
> `manifest_json.plugin_name == plugin.plugin_name`,
> `manifest_json.plugin_type == plugin.plugin_type`, and
> `manifest_json.labels == plugin.tags` **in the same order** (they are compared
> as canonical JSON arrays, not as sets, so reordering, a duplicate or an
> untrimmed label fails). Every violation returns the same opaque
> `400 VALIDATION_ERROR` with `details {"field":"body","reason":"invalid"}` and
> no hint as to which field disagreed — check all three before retrying.
>
> ```jsonc
> {
>   "plugin": {
>     "plugin_name": "deep-miner",
>     "plugin_type": "expert",
>     "visibility": "space",
>     "tags": ["research", "analysis"],
>     "manifest_json": {
>       "$schema": "cowork-plugin-manifest-2.0.json",
>       "plugin_name": "deep-miner",
>       "plugin_type": "expert",
>       "name": "deep-miner",
>       "description": "Research and analysis expert",
>       "labels": ["research", "analysis"],
>       "examples": []
>     },
>     "plugin_json": {
>       "$schema": "cowork-plugin-package-2.0.json",
>       "attachments": [{
>         "path": "AGENTS.md",
>         "content_type": "raw",
>         "mime_type": "text/markdown",
>         "raw_content": "# Deep Miner\n\nResearch the question and cite evidence."
>       }]
>     }
>   },
>   "relations": []
> }
> ```

> **Upsert replaces the row; it does not patch it.** The backend rebuilds the
> whole plugin from your document, preserving only `created_at`, the version
> history and the creator identity. An omitted `category_id`, `publisher` or
> `icon` is accepted as empty and **clears the stored value**. Read-modify-write
> with `plugin get` and resubmit the complete document on every edit. (`plugin
> import` is the opposite — it falls back to the existing row — so do not carry
> habits between the two paths.)

> **Every upsert replaces the relation set.** Always resubmit the complete list
> — including on metadata-only edits. Omitting the `relations` key is identical
> to submitting `[]` and soft-deletes every live relation (team members,
> attached skills, attached connectors) on save. Nothing validates this locally,
> so the destruction is silent. Echo `relation_id` for rows you want to keep
> verbatim.

After saving, publish with `plugin publish --plugin-id <id> --version <x.y.z>
--changelog "<matching release notes>"`. A private expert/team publishes
immediately. A Space-visible plugin follows the authenticated Space's
automatic-review policy: automatic approval is enabled by default when no
override is stored and publishes immediately; when disabled, it stays pending
for owner/admin approval. Follow the applicant or reviewer flow in `SKILL.md`
only for a pending request.

For an already-published Space expert/team upgrade, do not send the upsert
wrapper as the review body. `plugin review-request create --data
@submission.json` takes top-level `plugin_id` and `version`, plus the full
`manifest_json` and `plugin_json` objects together for the frozen replacement.
`changelog` is optional. Omit `relations` to inherit the live graph; when
supplied, include the complete replacement relation set. Use a strictly
forward-moving version label.

The review submission follows the same Space policy: when automatic approval
is enabled it can publish immediately; when disabled, it remains pending for
owner/admin approval.

`plugin import` is skill-only and must never be used to release an expert or
expert_team.

## Install into a Loop workspace

Before installing, ensure the active Marketplace caller can see every declared
dependency used by the install: an expert's skill plugins, or an expert_team's
member experts and each member's skill plugins. A visible root plugin does not
guarantee that its dependencies are visible. Install refuses a hidden declared
relation target rather than silently provisioning an incomplete expert/team.
The root's `plugin get --include-relations` output is only a one-level,
visibility-filtered view. Use `plugin graph get --plugin-id <id>` for the flat,
deduplicated transitive closure the caller can see (root → member experts → their
skills and connectors); do not infer transitive completeness from the one-level
read. Install-time resolution (frozen member and skill versions) happens
server-side.

Provision an expert or expert_team into a Loop workspace/runtime (creates the
agent or squad, acting as the caller against octo-fleet, with rollback):

```bash
octo-cli marketplace plugin install --plugin-id <id> --workspace-id <ws> --runtime-id <rt>
```

The response carries exactly one of `agent_id` (expert) or `squad_id`
(expert_team), depending on the plugin's type.

Optional runtime-specific environment is supplied as the `custom_env` string
map in `--data`, for example `--data @install.json` with
`{"custom_env":{"VARIABLE":"<value>"}}`. Treat values as secrets: the install
schema marks them for verbose/dry-run redaction, but redaction does not protect
the local body file or shell history.

## Delete (owner only, confirmed)

Soft delete; the name frees for reuse:

```bash
octo-cli marketplace plugin delete --plugin-id <id>
```

Skill packages attached to an expert/team are themselves `skill` plugins — to
release a new version of one, re-run the upload → parse → import flow in
[`skills.md`](skills.md) with the existing `--plugin-id`; wire or re-wire it
with an `expert_skill` relation in a subsequent `plugin upsert` that resubmits
the full relation list.
package service

import (
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"sort"
	"strings"

	"github.com/spf13/cobra"

	"github.com/Mininglamp-OSS/octo-cli/internal/cmdutil"
	"github.com/Mininglamp-OSS/octo-cli/internal/credential"
	"github.com/Mininglamp-OSS/octo-cli/internal/output"
	"github.com/Mininglamp-OSS/octo-cli/internal/registry"
)

// tokenKindNotAllowed is the machine code for "the selected credential is the
// wrong kind for this operation". It is a validation error (exit 2), matching
// the existing local gate in internal/client/search_route.go — the caller has
// to switch credentials, not re-authenticate the one they have.
const tokenKindNotAllowed = "TOKEN_KIND_NOT_ALLOWED"

// applyIdentityRouting enforces x-octo-allowed-token-kinds and applies
// x-octo-mount-by-token-kind to urlPath. Operations declaring neither
// extension are returned untouched and never resolve a credential here, so
// every pre-existing domain keeps its byte-identical path and its historical
// credential-resolution timing.
//
// Errors are already-classified *output.ExitError values:
//
//   - missing / ambiguous / unreadable credential → auth or validation, as the
//     credential chain classified it (exit 3 / 2)
//   - credential kind outside the allowed list, or absent from the mount table
//     → validation, TOKEN_KIND_NOT_ALLOWED (exit 2)
func applyIdentityRouting(f *cmdutil.Factory, d *registry.OperationDetail, urlPath string) (string, error) {
	if len(d.AllowedTokenKinds) == 0 && len(d.MountByTokenKind) == 0 {
		return urlPath, nil
	}

	kind, err := activeTokenKind(f)
	if err != nil {
		return "", err
	}

	if len(d.AllowedTokenKinds) > 0 && !containsString(d.AllowedTokenKinds, kind) {
		return "", tokenKindError(kind, d.AllowedTokenKinds)
	}
	if len(d.MountByTokenKind) == 0 {
		return urlPath, nil
	}
	mount, ok := d.MountByTokenKind[kind]
	if !ok {
		return "", tokenKindError(kind, mountKinds(d.MountByTokenKind))
	}
	return swapMount(urlPath, mount, d.MountByTokenKind)
}

// activeTokenKind resolves the credential and classifies it by token prefix.
// Resolution is cached on the Factory, and root's PersistentPreRunE has already
// forced it via cfg.Validate, so this adds no I/O — but ErrNoCredential must be
// translated here because buildConfig deliberately swallows it.
func activeTokenKind(f *cmdutil.Factory) (string, error) {
	cred, err := f.Credential()
	if err != nil {
		if errors.Is(err, credential.ErrNoCredential) {
			return "", output.ErrAuth(
				"no credential configured",
				"set OCTO_TOKEN (or OCTO_BOT_TOKEN), or select a stored profile with --bot-id / --profile")
		}
		return "", err
	}
	if cred == nil || cred.Token == "" {
		return "", output.ErrAuth(
			"no credential configured",
			"set OCTO_TOKEN (or OCTO_BOT_TOKEN), or select a stored profile with --bot-id / --profile")
	}
	return credential.TokenKind(cred.Token), nil
}

// tokenKindError reports an incompatible credential kind. The message names the
// kind actually in use so an Agent holding several credentials can tell which
// one it picked up.
func tokenKindError(kind string, allowed []string) *output.ExitError {
	got := kind
	if got == "" {
		got = "none"
	}
	return output.ErrWithHint(
		"validation",
		tokenKindNotAllowed,
		fmt.Sprintf("credential kind %q cannot run this operation (allowed: %s)", got, strings.Join(allowed, ", ")),
		"select a compatible credential with --bot-id / --profile, or set OCTO_TOKEN; `octo-cli auth list` shows stored profiles",
	)
}

// swapMount replaces the mount prefix the spec path is written with by the
// mount for the active token kind.
//
// The spec paths carry a full, real path (e.g. /v1/bot/drive/spaces) rather
// than a bare suffix, so `octo-cli schema` stays readable and the default
// token kind takes a zero-rewrite path. Which leading segment is the mount is
// therefore derived: exactly one value in the table must prefix the path, and
// the longest such match wins. A path that matches none is a spec bug and fails
// loudly rather than silently reaching a wrong URL.
func swapMount(urlPath, target string, table map[string]string) (string, error) {
	current := ""
	for _, candidate := range table {
		if !strings.HasPrefix(urlPath, candidate) {
			continue
		}
		if len(candidate) > len(current) {
			current = candidate
		}
	}
	if current == "" {
		return "", output.ErrWithHint(
			"internal",
			"MOUNT_PREFIX_UNKNOWN",
			fmt.Sprintf("operation path %q does not start with any x-octo-mount-by-token-kind prefix", urlPath),
			"operation-spec bug: write the path with one of the declared mounts",
		)
	}
	if current == target {
		return urlPath, nil
	}
	return target + strings.TrimPrefix(urlPath, current), nil
}

// mountKinds lists the token kinds a mount table covers, sorted so error
// messages are stable.
func mountKinds(table map[string]string) []string {
	kinds := make([]string, 0, len(table))
	for k := range table {
		kinds = append(kinds, k)
	}
	sort.Strings(kinds)
	return kinds
}

func containsString(list []string, want string) bool {
	for _, s := range list {
		if s == want {
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// MountForOperation resolves the server mount prefix that operationID's spec
// declares for the active credential's token kind, applying the same
// allowed-token-kinds gate the generated leaf commands use.
//
// Hand-written composite commands (drive upload file / download file / share
// create|blob-create|access|download) call this so they route and gate through
// exactly the same metadata as their generated siblings — there is no second
// copy of the mount table anywhere in the CLI.
func MountForOperation(f *cmdutil.Factory, operationID string) (string, error) {
	reg := f.Registry()
	if reg == nil {
		return "", output.ErrWithHint("internal", "REGISTRY_UNAVAILABLE",
			"operation registry is not available", "")
	}
	d, ok := reg.GetOperation(operationID)
	if !ok {
		return "", output.ErrWithHint("internal", "OPERATION_UNKNOWN",
			fmt.Sprintf("operation %q is not in the embedded registry", operationID),
			"CLI build bug: the composite command references a missing spec operation")
	}
	kind, err := activeTokenKind(f)
	if err != nil {
		return "", err
	}
	if len(d.AllowedTokenKinds) > 0 && !containsString(d.AllowedTokenKinds, kind) {
		return "", tokenKindError(kind, d.AllowedTokenKinds)
	}
	mount, ok := d.MountByTokenKind[kind]
	if !ok {
		return "", tokenKindError(kind, mountKinds(d.MountByTokenKind))
	}
	return mount, nil
}

// ResolveOperationIdentity resolves the active credential and applies d's
// token-kind gate without building a request: the same checks a generated
// leaf runs before its own --dry-run. The MCP multipart dry run, which plans
// the request from metadata instead of running the leaf, calls it so it never
// describes a request for a credential the real call would refuse.
func ResolveOperationIdentity(f *cmdutil.Factory, d *registry.OperationDetail) error {
	if _, err := activeTokenKind(f); err != nil {
		return err
	}
	_, err := applyIdentityRouting(f, d, d.Path)
	return err
}

// ValidateRequestBody applies operationID's declared pre-send body checks —
// required fields, minItems, enum vocabularies and uint64 id ranges — to a body
// a hand-written command assembled itself.
//
// A composite that replaces a generated leaf (drive share blob-create) or that
// posts to the same endpoint under another name (drive share create's blob
// branch) registers its own flags, so it does not inherit the engine's flag
// validation. Calling this keeps the spec the single source of the vocabulary
// either way: an out-of-enum --permission fails with ENUM_NOT_ALLOWED and exit 2
// before any HTTP, exactly as it does on the generated path, instead of the
// hand-written command sending it and letting the backend decide.
//
// flagFor maps a wire field name to the flag the caller typed, so the error
// names --permission rather than the JSON key behind it. Fields a composite
// takes positionally are simply absent from the map and are labelled by path.
func ValidateRequestBody(f *cmdutil.Factory, operationID string, body map[string]any, flagFor map[string]string) error {
	reg := f.Registry()
	if reg == nil {
		return output.ErrWithHint("internal", "REGISTRY_UNAVAILABLE",
			"operation registry is not available", "")
	}
	d, ok := reg.GetOperation(operationID)
	if !ok {
		return output.ErrWithHint("internal", "OPERATION_UNKNOWN",
			fmt.Sprintf("operation %q is not in the embedded registry", operationID),
			"CLI build bug: the composite command references a missing spec operation")
	}
	if d.RequestBody == nil {
		return nil
	}
	v := bodySchemaValidator{flagFor: flagFor}
	if exitErr := v.validate(d.RequestBody, body, "", ""); exitErr != nil {
		return exitErr
	}
	return nil
}

// RemoveLeaf detaches a generated leaf command by name from parent and reports
// whether it was there. Composite commands that must own a name the spec also
// exposes as a raw endpoint (drive share access / download / blob-create) call
// this before registering their own version, so the spec keeps documenting the
// endpoint for `octo-cli schema` while the command tree stays at one leaf per
// name.
func RemoveLeaf(parent *cobra.Command, name string) bool {
	if parent == nil {
		return false
	}
	for _, c := range parent.Commands() {
		if c.Name() == name {
			parent.RemoveCommand(c)
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// FindChild returns parent's subcommand with the given name, or nil. Exposed so
// hand-written commands can attach themselves under a generated subtree.
func FindChild(parent *cobra.Command, name string) *cobra.Command {
	return findChild(parent, name)
}
{"base_url_env":"OCTO_API_BASE_URL","description":"Reads th\u003cidx\u003e` / `${logicalId}:r\u003cidx\u003e` targets one tab, while bare `c\u003cidx\u003e` / `r\u003cidx\u003e` address"id":","type":"o,"type":"object"},"sheetMerges":{"description":"merged cell ranges keyed `${logicalId}:sr:sc:er:ec` (0-based startRow:startCol:endRow:endCol); each value is true. Returned on the first page only in paged mode.","type":"object"}},"type":"object"},"risk":"read","service"Read a spreadsheet's live cells, worksheet resources, and base version (needs reader)"}{"base_url_env":"OCTO_API_BASE_URL","description":"Reads th\u003cidx\u003e` / `${logicalId}:r\u003cidx\u003e` targets one tab, while bare `c\u003cidx\u003e` / `r\u003cidx\u003e` address"id":","type":"o,"type":"object"},"sheetMerges":{"description":"merged cell ranges keyed `${logicalId}:sr:sc:er:ec` (0-based startRow:startCol:endRow:endCol); each value is true. Returned on the first page only in paged mode.","type":"object"}},"type":"object"},"risk":"read","se# Stored-secret downloads over MCP

Use the dedicated `get_secret_download_url` tool with `{"query":"<alias-or-id>"}`.
It is present once in every facade: `three` has 4 tools, `two` has 3, and
`both` has 6. Existing meta-tools and their schemas retain their contracts.
The raw resolver is not a registry operation and cannot be called through
`execute` or `call_op`.

The server resolves under the actual caller's User Bot (`bf_`) credential.
The bot creator owns the secrets; the current chat actor and selected space
do not grant additional access. HTTP uses only its request bearer, with no
ambient credential fallback. Stdio uses ordinary credential/profile selection;
task mode remains prohibited for `mcp serve`. Unsupported credentials fail
with a fixed `auth_error` outcome. The existing Octo resolver enforces ownership.

`query` must be nonblank, at most 128 Unicode characters and 512 UTF-8 bytes.
The object is closed; unknown, mis-cased, duplicate or null fields are refused.
Optional `dry_run: true` and global `--dry-run` return a plan without credential
resolution, file access, resolver calls or Redis connections/writes.

Success contains only `status`, `download_url`, `expires_at` (UTC), and
`expires_in_seconds`. An ambiguous alias returns only candidate `secret_id`
and `display_name`: ask the user to choose explicitly, then retry the ID.
Never guess. Other outcomes are fixed `validation_error`, `auth_error`,
`not_found`, `unavailable` or `busy`; upstream error bodies are discarded.

Download directly to a private **CLIENT file** using an ordinary downloader.
Never use text-extraction fetch or `resources/read` to display the key. Never
read/open/cat the file into model context. Scripts/code may read it internally,
but must never echo secrets or expose them through tracing, stdout, stderr,
exceptions or error output. Pass a file path to the consumer; do not paste
the file contents into chat, source, configuration examples or command arguments.

The URL itself is a sensitive temporary credential. Do not display or persist
it publicly, attach it to issues, or enable link previews/unfurling. Host tool
history can retain it: these instructions and file permissions are not hard
isolation from the same host identity or a model able to run arbitrary code.
Downloaded files and history are not erased when the URL expires.

This generic Bash/curl example contains placeholders only. Supply the returned
URL through a protected input channel, with shell tracing disabled. It keeps
the URL out of curl's argv, creates a private directory/file, never overwrites
an existing destination, suppresses body/error diagnostics, rejects redirects,
and removes partial files. It does not install a new client or SDK.

```bash
set +x
set -eu
umask 077
SECRET_DOWNLOAD_URL='<temporary-https-url-from-tool>'
# The tool emits only this URL shape. Refuse config-file metacharacters.
case "$SECRET_DOWNLOAD_URL" in
  *'"'*|*'\'*|*$'\n'*|*$'\r'*) exit 1 ;;
  https://*/mcp/secret-download/*) ;;
  *) exit 1 ;;
esac
private_dir=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/octo-secret.XXXXXXXX")
partial="$private_dir/.partial"
destination="$private_dir/secret.bin"
trap 'rm -f "$partial"; rmdir "$private_dir" 2>/dev/null || true' EXIT
if ! http_status=$(printf 'url = "%s"\n' "$SECRET_DOWNLOAD_URL" |
  curl --config - --proto '=https' --silent --fail --max-time 10 \
    --output "$partial" --write-out '%{http_code}' 2>/dev/null); then
  exit 1
fi
[ "$http_status" = 200 ] || exit 1
# Hard-link creation is exclusive: an existing destination is never replaced.
ln "$partial" "$destination"
unset SECRET_DOWNLOAD_URL
printf '%s\n' "$destination"   # path only; no automatic open/read
```

Run the intended consumer with this private path and remove the file after use.
Keep tracing/debug and secret-bearing error output disabled in the consumer.
An aborted GET may be retried with the same link until expiry; request a new
link after expiry. Never print a response body to diagnose a failed download.

The link holds a **snapshot selected at issuance**. Rotation, deletion or
revocation of the original stored secret does not update or delete existing
snapshots. A new issuance resolves the current value and authorization. The
link is reusable, including concurrent GETs, until admission expiry. HEAD is
405 without a body; Range and marked Purpose/Sec-Purpose/X-Purpose requests
are refused. Unmarked preview GETs cannot be distinguished from downloads.
No resume, redirect, extra Octo bearer or client SDK is required.

## Operator configuration and deployment prerequisites

These are deployment requirements, not a claim that Redis or HTTPS is deployed.
No octo-server changes or object storage are involved.

| Environment variable | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `OCTO_MCP_SECRET_REDIS_URL` | Private replica-accessible `redis://` or `rediss://` URL; optional ACL username/password and nonnegative DB. Query/fragment options are refused. Never log the DSN. Use authenticated TLS (`rediss://`, certificate verification, TLS 1.2+) on nonlocal networks. |
| `OCTO_MCP_SECRET_REDIS_PREFIX` | Default `octo:mcp:secret-download:`; 1–128 ASCII letters/digits/colon/underscore/hyphen. Reserve an exclusive ACL key namespace. Every publisher/reader must share it. |
| `OCTO_MCP_SECRET_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` | Explicit client-reachable HTTPS origin only, with no credentials/path/query/fragment. HTTP numeric loopback is allowed only for isolated fixtures. Request Host/forwarded headers never construct links. |
| `OCTO_MCP_SECRET_TTL` | Positive Go duration, default `300s`, minimum `1ms`, maximum Go `time.Duration` (`9223372036854775807ns`, truncated to milliseconds). Recommend at most `600s`; `601s` is accepted. No caller TTL override. |

Run an HTTP MCP service (`octo-cli mcp serve --http 127.0.0.1:<port>`) behind
a TLS proxy routing `/mcp/secret-download/<capability>` without redirects.
Every HTTP replica shares Redis/prefix. For stdio, run that same HTTP service
separately and configure the stdio publisher with its public origin and the
same Redis/prefix. Stdio writes the snapshot into Redis; the HTTP service
serves it. Stdio needs no hidden local listener and must not advertise an
unreachable loopback URL. Provisioning that service/routing is explicit.

Unset, partial or invalid config disables this feature with `unavailable`;
existing MCP tools continue working. A Redis outage also fails closed. Startup
does not connect to Redis or resolve secrets. No real credential configuration
is read or changed by the isolated test fixtures.

Each capability uses 256 random bits; Redis keys store its SHA-256 hash, never
the capability or a long-lived Octo bearer. A single atomic Redis script writes
the base64-encoded snapshot and absolute deadline with `SET NX PX`; there is
no record-without-TTL window. Redis time governs issuance and admission across
replicas. Every GET checks the absolute deadline and positive physical TTL
before reading bytes, independently of asynchronous reclamation. Eviction,
restart without persistence, or expiry makes the link unavailable. A GET
admitted before expiry may finish within a 10-second write deadline. Aborting
a transfer does not consume the record; failed/aborted issuance may leave a
bounded orphan until its TTL expires. Transport retries are disabled.

Bounds per process: 16 concurrent issuance/download operations (overflow is
refused), Redis pool maximum 16 connections, resolver value 8 KiB, resolver
body 64 KiB, headers 16 KiB, stored record 16 KiB on retrieval, dedicated tool
argument object 2 KiB, and existing RPC envelope 8 MiB. Each operation has a
10-second deadline; Redis dial/read/write/pool waits have 2-second bounds;
resolver response headers have 5 seconds. Payloads are held in bounded memory,
never written to server disk. Fixed response headers include octet-stream,
attachment `secret.bin`, no-store, nosniff and no-referrer; bytes have no added
newline. Invalid/expired/missing downloads share a fixed 404 error. The endpoint
does not forward Authorization to Octo or contact Octo at download time.

Redis must be private, access controlled, memory bounded, and protected from
unbounded issuance through edge rate limits suited to the deployment. The
resolver's existing backend limits still apply. Provision a dedicated
ephemeral Redis with persistence/backups disabled when retained plaintext is
unacceptable. The stored base64 payload is **not encrypted**. Redis AOF/RDB,
replication, snapshots and backups can retain values beyond TTL; TTL is not
secure erasure. An optional operator-shared encryption key could reduce Redis
backup exposure but adds key distribution/rotation and does not protect a
compromised MCP process. This feature does not introduce that dependency.
If policy requires encrypted-at-rest payloads, resolve that operator decision
before deployment rather than treating TLS/TTL as at-rest encryption.

Disable access/recovery/body/command logging and APM payload capture for this
route and the dedicated issuance tool, including the TLS proxy/CDN. Never log
full URLs, tokens, resolver bodies, Redis command arguments or DSNs. The MCP
implementation emits no such diagnostics; third-party infrastructure and
client history require separate verification. Do not cache this route.
Independent real caller authorization, cross-host HTTPS, routing, logging,
preview/indexing and operator retention checks are required before acceptance.
package mcp

import (
	"bytes"
	"context"
	"encoding/json"
	"errors"
	"strings"
	"sync"
	"sync/atomic"
	"time"

	"github.com/Mininglamp-OSS/octo-cli/internal/cmdutil"
)

// Admission is shared only for bounded capacity, the immutable operator policy
// source, and the single request writer. It stores no per-Bot identity cache.
type Admission struct {
	stopped              atomic.Bool
	log                  *RequestLog
	debug                *DebugPolicy
	tools, verifications chan struct{}
	mu                   sync.Mutex
	tokens               float64
	updated              time.Time
}

// NewAdmission fixes the operator policy source and starts the request writer.
func NewAdmission(logDirectory string, debug *DebugPolicy) (*Admission, error) {
	l, err := OpenRequestLog(logDirectory)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return &Admission{log: l, debug: debug, tools: make(chan struct{}, 32), verifications: make(chan struct{}, 16), tokens: 16, updated: time.Now()}, nil
}

// Close stops new verification and drains the bounded request writer.
func (a *Admission) Close() error {
	if a == nil {
		return nil
	}
	a.stopped.Store(true)
	return a.log.Close()
}

// WithAdmission binds operator-owned admission before serving requests.
func (s *Server) WithAdmission(a *Admission) *Server { s.admission = a; return s }
func (a *Admission) verificationSlot() (func(), error) {
	if a.stopped.Load() {
		return nil, logUnavailable()
	}
	a.mu.Lock()
	now := time.Now()
	a.tokens += now.Sub(a.updated).Seconds() * 8
	if a.tokens > 16 {
		a.tokens = 16
	}
	a.updated = now
	if a.tokens < 1 {
		a.mu.Unlock()
		return nil, admissionFailure(-32006, "MCP_BUSY")
	}
	select {
	case a.verifications <- struct{}{}:
		a.tokens--
		a.mu.Unlock()
		return func() { <-a.verifications }, nil
	default:
		a.mu.Unlock()
		return nil, admissionFailure(-32006, "MCP_BUSY")
	}
}

func admissionResponse(id json.RawMessage, err error) rpcResponse {
	var ce *contextError
	if errors.As(err, &ce) {
		r := newErrorResponse(id, codeInvalidParams, "invalid Octo context")
		r.Error.Data = map[string]string{"field_path": ce.path, "reason": ce.reason}
		return r
	}
	var ae *admissionError
	if errors.As(err, &ae) {
		return newErrorResponse(id, ae.code, ae.reason)
	}
	return newErrorResponse(id, codeInvalidParams, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
}

func (s *Server) verify(ctx context.Context, policy bool) (call *Server, uid, origin, mode string, err error) {
	a := s.admission
	if a == nil {
		return nil, "", "", "", logUnavailable()
	}
	release, err := a.verificationSlot()
	if err != nil {
		return nil, "", "", "", err
	}
	defer release()
	fn := s.factoryFn
	if fn == nil {
		fn = s.makeFactory
	}
	f, out, errout := fn(s.trusted)
	uid, origin, mode, err = verifyMCPBot(ctx, f, policy)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, "", "", "", err
	}
	bot, err := f.Credential()
	if err != nil {
		return nil, "", "", "", admissionFailure(-32001, "BOT_AUTH_INVALID")
	}
	// Build a new server explicitly: mutexes and protocol negotiation state
	// must never be copied or used as request-scoped provenance storage.
	s.mu.Lock()
	clientResources := s.clientResources
	s.mu.Unlock()
	call = &Server{reg: s.reg, mapping: s.mapping, build: s.build, trusted: s.trusted, credentialToken: bot.Token, httpMode: s.httpMode, uploadRoot: s.uploadRoot, allowLocalUpload: s.allowLocalUpload, uploadOpener: s.uploadOpener, baseGlobals: s.baseGlobals, facade: s.facade, secretDownloads: s.secretDownloads, admission: a, protocolVersion: defaultProtocolVersion, clientResources: clientResources}
	call.factoryFn = func(TrustedContext) (*cmdutil.Factory, *bytes.Buffer, *bytes.Buffer) { return f, out, errout }
	return call, uid, origin, mode, nil
}

func (s *Server) admitToolCall(ctx context.Context, req rpcRequest, p toolCallParams, received time.Time) (*Server, func(), error) { //nolint:gocritic,gocyclo // immutable request snapshots are copied at this boundary; ordered authentication, policy, context, exposure and durability gates must all precede execution
	a := s.admission
	if a == nil || a.stopped.Load() {
		return nil, nil, logUnavailable()
	}
	select {
	case a.tools <- struct{}{}:
	default:
		return nil, nil, admissionFailure(-32006, "MCP_BUSY")
	}
	release := func() { <-a.tools }
	call, uid, origin, mode, err := s.verify(ctx, a.debug == nil)
	if err != nil {
		release()
		return nil, nil, err
	}
	if a.debug != nil {
		mode, err = a.debug.resolve(uid, origin)
		if err != nil {
			release()
			return nil, nil, err
		}
	}
	source, err := parseDeclaredSource(p.Meta)
	if err != nil {
		release()
		return nil, nil, err
	}
	if mode == "strict" && source == nil {
		release()
		return nil, nil, admissionFailure(-32602, "CONTEXT_REQUIRED")
	}
	if s.trusted.OnBehalfOf != "" {
		release()
		return nil, nil, admissionFailure(-32005, "OBO_FORBIDDEN")
	}
	var controls struct {
		OperationID string `json:"operation_id"`
	}
	if len(p.Arguments) > 0 {
		if err := json.Unmarshal(p.Arguments, &controls); err != nil {
			release()
			return nil, nil, admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
		}
	}
	var ordinary map[string]bool
	if controls.OperationID == "docs.comment-task.get" || controls.OperationID == "docs.comment-task.events" {
		ordinary = map[string]bool{"botUid": true}
	}
	if err = guardBotOnlyArguments(ctx, p.Arguments, ordinary); err != nil {
		release()
		return nil, nil, err
	}
	if oboOperation(controls.OperationID) {
		release()
		return nil, nil, admissionFailure(-32005, "OBO_FORBIDDEN")
	}
	if detail, ok := s.reg.GetOperation(controls.OperationID); ok {
		if s.reg.ServiceDisabled(detail.Service) || !mcpExposed(controls.OperationID) {
			// The original handlers return the established safe error envelope
			// at these gates, before any execution or request-store access.
			return call, release, nil
		}
		if _, div := divergentMCPOps[controls.OperationID]; div {
			return call, release, nil
		}
		// Header declarations can carry auth controls even when their body
		// spelling would be ordinary business data. Deny such operations.
		for i := range detail.Parameters {
			param := &detail.Parameters[i]
			if param.In == "header" && identityControl(param.Name) {
				release()
				return nil, nil, admissionFailure(-32005, "IDENTITY_OVERRIDE_FORBIDDEN")
			}
		}
	}
	record, err := projectRequest(call, req, p, uid, call.credentialToken, source, received)
	if err == nil {
		err = a.log.AppendAndSync(ctx, record)
	}
	if err == nil && ctx.Err() != nil {
		err = logUnavailable()
	}
	if err != nil {
		release()
		return nil, nil, err
	}
	return call, release, nil
}

func oboOperation(id string) bool { return id == "bot.obo-grant" || id == "bot.obo-grants-self" }

func guardOperationArguments(ctx context.Context, id string, args map[string]any) error {
	if oboOperation(id) {
		return admissionFailure(-32005, "OBO_FORBIDDEN")
	}
	raw, err := json.Marshal(map[string]any{"arguments": args})
	if err != nil {
		return admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
	}
	var ordinary map[string]bool
	if id == "docs.comment-task.get" || id == "docs.comment-task.events" {
		ordinary = map[string]bool{"botUid": true}
	}
	return guardBotOnlyArguments(ctx, raw, ordinary)
}
func controlKey(k string) string {
	return strings.ToLower(safetyNameKey(k))
}
func identityControl(k string) bool {
	switch controlKey(k) {
	case "token", "authorization", "profile", "botid", "credential", "actoruid", "subjectuid", "identitymode", "policydebug", "debugpolicyfile", "policydebugfile", "contextmode", "botuid", "forceonbehalfof":
		return true
	}
	return false
}

func guardBotOnlyArguments(ctx context.Context, raw json.RawMessage, ordinary ...map[string]bool) error { //nolint:gocyclo // bounded recursive scanner checks each reserved-control class before map decoding
	keys := 0
	var walk func(json.RawMessage, int) error
	walk = func(v json.RawMessage, depth int) error {
		if depth > 64 {
			return admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
		}
		v = bytes.TrimSpace(v)
		if len(v) == 0 {
			return nil
		}
		if v[0] == '{' {
			seen := make(map[string]bool)
			return visitObjectMembers(v, func(k string, value json.RawMessage) error {
				if seen[k] {
					return admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
				}
				seen[k] = true
				keys++
				if keys > 8192 {
					return admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
				}
				if keys%64 == 0 && ctx.Err() != nil {
					return admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
				}
				folded := controlKey(k)
				if folded == "onbehalfof" || folded == "actualsenderuid" || strings.HasPrefix(folded, "obo") {
					return admissionFailure(-32005, "OBO_FORBIDDEN")
				}
				// Outer tool controls and operation-level identity selectors are
				// reserved; ordinary nested business fields are not identities.
				businessFilter := depth == 1 && len(ordinary) > 0 && ordinary[0][k]
				if depth <= 1 && identityControl(k) && !businessFilter {
					return admissionFailure(-32005, "IDENTITY_OVERRIDE_FORBIDDEN")
				}
				return walk(value, depth+1)
			})
		}
		if v[0] == '[' {
			var a []json.RawMessage
			if json.Unmarshal(v, &a) != nil {
				return admissionFailure(-32602, "INVALID_ARGUMENTS")
			}
			for _, child := range a {
				if err := walk(child, depth+1); err != nil {
					return err
				}
			}
		}
		return nil
	}
	return walk(raw, 0)
}
